' =====================================================================
' RoboFetch - A7: the prediction service cannot be reached, and the
' system decides anyway. This is NFR2 demonstrated rather than claimed.
'
' Predictor.__call__ returns None on timeout, connection failure, a
' missing model or a malformed reply. decide() then leaves decided_by
' as "policy" and the four physical gates carry the decision alone.
' The 1.5 s client timeout sits inside NFR1's 2 s preview budget on
' purpose: a hanging AI must not blow the response-time requirement.
' =====================================================================
@startuml
skinparam backgroundColor White
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #34495E
  LifeLineBorderColor #7F8C8D
  ParticipantBorderColor #34495E
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FBFCFE
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #EAF2FA
}

actor Controller as C
participant "Web tier" as API
database "SQLite" as DB
participant "AI service\n(down)" as AI
participant "Task manager" as TM

== Cost the job, exactly as before ==
C -> API ++ : POST /preview  SKU-1002 -> delivery_1
API -> DB ++ : product, pick point, bay
DB --> API -- : 0.6 kg, pick (-2.0, 0.95), bay (-3.0, -2.2)
API -> DB ++ : live robot condition
DB --> API -- : battery 92%, 24 C, condition 100%
API -> API : project, then cost the three legs
API -> API : gates 1-4 all pass

== The model cannot be reached ==
API ->x AI : POST /predict  (1.5 s timeout)
API -> API : Predictor returns None;\ndecided_by stays "policy"
API --> C -- : ACCEPTED  decided_by = policy\ncost shown as usual

== The order runs normally ==
C -> API ++ : POST /confirm
API -> DB : create order, record decided_by = policy
API ->> TM : publish /orders/new
API --> C -- : order pending

note over API, AI
  NFR2: the robot must keep working when the AI does not. The four
  physical gates are unchanged, so nothing unsafe can be admitted -
  the model was only ever able to refuse, never to approve. The order
  records that it was decided without the model, so the report can say
  how each decision was actually made.
end note

@enduml
